    "product_id": "6ba3f21a-c9a9-44b7-a3c7-a22d7ab904cd",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 251223\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n423 AM PST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Breezy north winds today and Thursday along the Colorado River \n  Valley; otherwise, winds remain light through the weekend.\n\n* A warming trend is expected the rest of the week with above-normal \n  temperatures Thursday through the weekend. \n\n* A potentially substantial storm system may impact the first half\n  of next week. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Early Next Week.\n\nA shortwave will push through the region today and tonight. While \nthis feature will remain dry due to limited moisture, north winds \nare expected to increase in the Colorado River Valley, especially in \nsouthern portions of the valley. Wind gusts 20-30 MPH are generally \nexpected, peaking tonight and Thursday early morning. Gusts over 35 \nMPH are possible, but will be isolated to around Bullhead City \n/ Laughlin and will not be long-lived. Given the isolated nature of \nthe stronger winds, a Lake Wind Advisory has not been issued, though \nhazardous boating conditions may be possible briefly Thursday \nmorning on Lake Mohave. Winds will diminish Thursday afternoon and \nremain lighter through the weekend. Outside the Colorado River \nValley, winds will remain below impactful levels.\n\nA warming trend is expected through the week as in general, ridging \nwill dominate the weather pattern outside of a few quick moving weak \npieces of energy. Temperatures today will continue run a few degrees \nbelow-normal, but will be warmer than yesterday. By Thursday, above \nnormal temperatures are expected. Temperatures will remain 5-8 \ndegrees above-normal through the rest of the week, including Sunday \ndespite another shortwave moving through the region. This shortwave \nwill also lack moisture and will be rather weak, so the only \npotential result would be breezy winds Sunday in the Western Mojave \nDesert as dry conditions and mild temperatures persist.\n\nModels continue to show a potential more potent system early next \nweek. Timing continues to be a questions with the latest suite of \nmodels bringing it through slower and focused late Tuesday and \nWednesday. Track will also be important as where this system does \nmove through (and at what strength) while be where the bulk of the \nmoisture will move in. The latest AR probabilities show a slight \nnorthward shift with the better moisture. This will all likely \ncontinue to adjust as we get closer so its too early to give exact \ndetails or impacts. Widespread rain chances in the current forecast \nlooks good at the moment as moisture and forcing should be enough to \novertop the Sierra, but will continue to monitor with latest \ninformation. With shifts in the track, wind impacts will also be \nsomething to monitor. \n&&\n\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Winds \nwill remain light through the TAF period, favoring typical daily \ndirectional trends. SCT-BKN aoa 25 kft will continue through the day \nbefore thinning out to FEW-SCT in the evening.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Light winds are expected \nacross most of the region with SCT-BKN aoa 25 kft persisting through \nthe afternoon. The exception to the light winds will be along the \nColorado River Valley near KIFP and KEED where breezy north winds \nwith gusts of 20-30 kts are expected to pick up this morning and \ncontinue through the remainder of the TAF period.\n&&\n\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n&&\n\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Nickerson\nAVIATION...Soulat\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
